<!-- /end intro --><div class="rs_skip"><a class="download-btn print-hide" href="article/download/121536" id="download-pdf">Download Article <span>[PDF]</span></a>&nbsp;</div>        <br clear="all"/><br/>

        <div class="copy">
                        <div class="article-inner_html">
                <p>Wenqian Nong,<sup>1,</sup><sup>&ast;</sup> Junhao Zhang,<sup>2,</sup><sup>&ast;</sup> Jun Zhou,<sup>3</sup> Li Liang,<sup>4</sup> Jiali Liu,<sup>5</sup> Haimei Lun,<sup>2</sup> Lingling Li,<sup>2</sup> Yunxia Deng,<sup>2</sup> Qiao Hu<sup>2</sup><br/><br/><sup>1</sup>Basic Medical Sciences Center, Guangxi Academy of Medical Sciences and the People’s Hospital of Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region, Nanning, 530021, People’s Republic of China; <sup>2</sup>Department of Ultrasound, Guangxi Academy of Medical Sciences and the People’s Hospital of Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region, Nanning, 530021, People’s Republic of China; <sup>3</sup>Department of Ultrasound, The Second People’s Hospital of China Three Gorges University, Yichang, 443000, People’s Republic of China; <sup>4</sup>Department of Ultrasound, The First People’s Hospital of Chenzhou, Chenzhou, 423000, People’s Republic of China; <sup>5</sup>Department of Ultrasound, Kailuan General Hospital, Tangshan, 063000, People’s Republic of China<br/><br/>&ast;These authors contributed equally to this work<br/><br/>Correspondence: Qiao Hu, Email <a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="e59d9c9c9d9c8d94a5d4d3d6cb868a88">[email&#160;protected]</a><br/><br/><strong>Purpose:</strong> To address the limitations in the effectiveness of chemotherapy for laryngeal cancer, including insufficient tumor-selective accumulation, uncontrolled drug release from nanocarriers, and the chemoresistance-associated hypoxic tumor microenvironment, we developed an iRGD-functionalized phase-change liposomal nanodroplet, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, for ultrasound imaging-guided chemotherapy and hypoxia modulation.<br/><strong>Methods:</strong> iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was constructed as an iRGD-modified liposomal nanoplatform co-encapsulating doxorubicin (DOX), catalase (CAT), and perfluoropentane (PFP). In this system, iRGD was used to enhance tumor targeting and cellular uptake, PFP served as an ultrasound-responsive phase-transition core for contrast enhancement and triggered drug release, CAT was incorporated to decompose endogenous H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> and improve local oxygen availability. The nanodroplets were evaluated for their physicochemical properties, phase-transition behavior, ultrasound imaging performance, drug release characteristics, cellular uptake, cytotoxicity, hypoxia modulation, and antitumor efficacy through a series of in vitro and in vivo experiments.<br/><strong>Results:</strong> iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP exhibited a mean hydrodynamic diameter of 236.10 ± 9.28 nm, good colloidal stability, and efficient cellular internalization in TU686 laryngeal cancer cells. Under low-intensity focused ultrasound (LIFU) irradiation, the nanodroplets underwent acoustic droplet vaporization, resulting in enhanced ultrasound contrast and accelerated DOX release. In vitro, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP combined with LIFU exhibited greater cytotoxicity than the non-phase-change iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX formulation, with IC<sub>50</sub> values of 3.27 and 11.27 μg/mL, respectively. In vivo, the iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU group showed significantly lower pimonidazole signals than the free DOX, lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU, and iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP + LIFU groups (<em>p</em> < 0.01), accompanied by reduced HIF-1α expression. The complete formulation combined with LIFU also produced the greatest inhibition of tumor growth among the tested groups (<em>p</em> < 0.001).<br/><strong>Conclusion:</strong> This multifunctional liposomal nanosystem integrates tumor-targeted chemotherapy, hypoxia modulation, LIFU-triggered drug release, and ultrasound imaging capability within a single platform. By enabling spatially controlled drug release together with ultrasound imaging guidance, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP provides a promising strategy for localized treatment of laryngeal cancer.<br/><br/><strong>Keywords:</strong> laryngeal cancer, phase-change nanodroplet, ultrasound-guided therapy, low-intensity focused ultrasound.</p>                        <div id="article-fulltext" class="html-oct">
                            <div class="$body">
<h2>Introduction</h2>
<p>Laryngeal cancer remains a major head and neck malignancy because disease progression threatens not only survival but also essential functions such as voice preservation and swallowing.<sup><a href="#cit0001" id="ref-cit0001">1</a></sup> Despite advances in systemic chemotherapy, treatment efficacy in solid tumors remains limited because only a small fraction of intravenously administered nanoparticles reaches tumor tissues, often estimated to be below 1% of the injected dose.<sup><a href="#cit0002" id="ref-cit0002">2</a></sup> In particular, the anatomical complexity of laryngeal tumors and the presence of a heterogeneous tumor microenvironment create additional challenges for achieving sufficient intratumoral drug accumulation while minimizing damage to surrounding normal tissues. Therefore, therapeutic strategies capable of improving tumor-specific delivery and overcoming microenvironment-associated resistance are highly desirable for laryngeal cancer treatment.</p>
<p>Nanocarrier-based delivery systems have been developed to improve drug solubility, circulation stability, and tumor accumulation. However, despite these advantages, many nanomedicines still exhibit limited therapeutic efficiency because passive accumulation through the enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect is highly heterogeneous among tumors.<sup><a href="#cit0003" id="ref-cit0003">3</a></sup> Even so, conventional carriers still face several practical barriers. Passive accumulation alone rarely guarantees efficient entry into tumor cells, and premature leakage during circulation can lower the amount of active drug that ultimately reaches the tumor.<sup><a href="#cit0004" id="ref-cit0004">4</a></sup> Once within the tumor, many carriers also struggle with incomplete cellular internalization, insufficient intracellular release, and poor access to relevant subcellular targets.<sup><a href="#cit0005" id="ref-cit0005">5</a></sup> In addition, most delivery systems act only as therapeutic vehicles and provide little real-time information on whether they have accumulated or been activated at the intended site.<sup><a href="#cit0006" id="ref-cit0006">6</a></sup> However, improving drug delivery alone may not be sufficient, because the therapeutic outcome of chemotherapy is also strongly influenced by tumor penetration, intracellular drug release, and resistance-associated microenvironmental factors.</p>
<p>Among various chemotherapeutic agents, doxorubicin (DOX) has been widely investigated in liposomal drug delivery systems because of its favorable compatibility with lipid-based nanocarriers.<sup><a href="#cit0007" id="ref-cit0007">7</a></sup> Unlike some conventional chemotherapeutic regimens used in laryngeal cancer, such as docetaxel/cisplatin/5-fluorouracil (TPF)-based chemotherapy, which are primarily optimized for systemic administration and are often limited by cumulative toxicity and insufficient tumor-selective exposure,<sup><a href="#cit0008" id="ref-cit0008">8</a></sup> DOX provides a suitable model drug for evaluating controlled nanomedicine delivery strategies. The amphiphilic characteristics of DOX allow efficient incorporation into lipid bilayers and aqueous compartments of liposomal systems, while clinically validated liposomal DOX formulations have demonstrated improved drug retention, altered biodistribution, and reduced systemic toxicity compared with free DOX.<sup><a href="#cit0009" id="ref-cit0009">9</a></sup> Therefore, DOX was selected in this study as a representative chemotherapeutic payload to investigate the ability of the proposed liposomal nanodroplets to achieve tumor-targeted accumulation, ultrasound-triggered release, and enhanced therapeutic efficacy.</p>
<p>Beyond improving drug delivery efficiency, an ideal therapeutic platform should also enable precise spatial and temporal control over drug activation to minimize off-target effects. Among external stimuli, ultrasound is particularly attractive for tumor therapy because it can penetrate several centimeters into biological tissues, provide real-time imaging guidance, and trigger localized therapeutic activation without the limited penetration depth associated with light-based modalities.<sup><a href="#cit0010" id="ref-cit0010">10</a></sup> Unlike imaging modalities that only provide anatomical information, ultrasound can actively manipulate therapeutic nanomaterials through mechanical effects, including acoustic droplet vaporization, membrane permeabilization, and stimulus-responsive drug release. At the same time, endogenous tumor-associated stimuli remain valuable because they can provide an additional level of responsiveness that is intrinsic to the tumor microenvironment. Nanodroplets that respond to both endogenous and exogenous cues may offer more precise and efficient drug delivery than systems that rely on a single trigger. Importantly, ultrasound is not only used as an imaging modality but also acts as an external trigger to induce acoustic droplet vaporization (ADV) of perfluorocarbon-containing nanodroplets, generating microbubbles and facilitating localized drug release.</p>
<p>Among ultrasound-responsive systems, phase-change nanodroplets are particularly attractive because they convert acoustic energy into a localized phase transition response. Perfluoropentane (PFP)-based nanodroplets are particularly advantageous because PFP remains in a liquid state at physiological temperature but undergoes ultrasound-induced vaporization to generate gas microbubbles, resulting in enhanced echogenicity and mechanical disruption of carrier structures.<sup><a href="#cit0011" id="ref-cit0011">11</a></sup> Upon LIFU exposure, liquid PFP nanodroplets can rapidly transform into gas microbubbles through ADV, resulting in enhanced acoustic signals and facilitating controlled drug release at the tumor site.<sup><a href="#cit0012" id="ref-cit0012">12</a></sup> At the same time, drug liberation from liposomal systems may also be influenced by the acidic tumor microenvironment. Acidic extracellular pH is a well-recognized feature of many solid tumors and is closely linked to hypoxia, increased glycolysis, and lactate accumulation.<sup><a href="#cit0013" id="ref-cit0013">13</a></sup> Recent studies further indicate that tumor acidity is not spatially uniform, which helps explain why pH-responsive systems alone may perform inconsistently in vivo.<sup><a href="#cit0014" id="ref-cit0014">14</a></sup> For this reason, nanodroplets that combine pH sensitivity with ultrasound-triggered phase transition are particularly appealing for controlled cancer therapy.</p>
<p>However, despite the advantages of ultrasound-responsive phase-change systems, efficient tumor accumulation after systemic administration remains a critical challenge. Therefore, incorporating active targeting strategies may further improve the delivery efficiency of these nanoplatforms. The tumor-penetrating peptide iRGD has received considerable attention because of its ability to enhance tumor accumulation and intracellular delivery of nanocarriers. Initially, iRGD binds to integrins such as &#x03B1;v&#x03B2;3 and &#x03B1;v&#x03B2;5, which are frequently overexpressed on tumor cells and tumor-associated vasculature.<sup><a href="#cit0015" id="ref-cit0015">15</a></sup> Following proteolytic cleavage, the C-terminal CendR motif (CRGDK/R) is exposed and subsequently interacts with neuropilin-1 (NRP-1), activating a NRP-1-dependent internalization pathway.<sup><a href="#cit0016" id="ref-cit0016">16</a></sup> This receptor-mediated process promotes tissue penetration and facilitates intracellular uptake of iRGD-modified nanoparticles. iRGD-based targeting has already shown feasibility in head and neck cancer models, including laryngeal carcinoma. For example, iRGD-modified ICG-loaded liposomes have been used for fluorescence-guided photothermal/photodynamic treatment of laryngeal carcinoma.<sup><a href="#cit0017" id="ref-cit0017">17</a></sup></p>
<p>Hypoxia represents another important barrier in laryngeal cancer.<sup><a href="#cit0018" id="ref-cit0018">18</a></sup> As tumors enlarge, rapid cellular proliferation and oxygen consumption can exceed the supply provided by the abnormal tumor vasculature, producing heterogeneous hypoxic regions.<sup><a href="#cit0019" id="ref-cit0019">19</a></sup> Elevated HIF-1&#x03B1; expression has been documented in laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma and has been associated with tumor progression and adverse clinical features.<sup><a href="#cit0020" id="ref-cit0020">20</a></sup> Hypoxia can also reduce chemotherapy sensitivity through HIF-1&#x03B1;-dependent mechanisms, including enhanced drug-efflux activity and altered apoptotic responses, and reduced sensitivity to DOX has been reported under hypoxic conditions.<sup><a href="#cit0019" id="ref-cit0019">19</a></sup> Catalase (CAT) therefore provides a biologically relevant strategy for modulating this microenvironment because it can convert endogenous H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> into O<sub>2</sub>,<sup><a href="#cit0021" id="ref-cit0021">21</a></sup> potentially improving local oxygen availability and reducing hypoxia-associated therapeutic resistance.</p>
<p>Several related nanoplatforms have established important foundations for ultrasound-responsive drug delivery and tumor microenvironment modulation. Lin et al developed PFP-containing emulsion liposomes co-loaded with DOX and demonstrated ultrasound-triggered DOX release, establishing the feasibility of combining a volatile perfluorocarbon with liposomal chemotherapy for externally controlled drug delivery.<sup><a href="#cit0022" id="ref-cit0022">22</a></sup> Subsequently, PFP- and DOX-loaded echogenic heat-sensitive liposomes were developed for ultrasound-imageable, HIFU-assisted drug delivery.<sup><a href="#cit0023" id="ref-cit0023">23</a></sup> CAT-based nanoplatforms have also been investigated to improve oxygen availability during cancer therapy; for example, catalase-containing nanoparticles have been used to generate O<sub>2</sub> from endogenous H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> and enhance ultrasound-guided focused ultrasound treatment combined with DOX chemotherapy.<sup><a href="#cit0024" id="ref-cit0024">24</a></sup> More recently, iRGD-functionalized PFP-containing phase-transitional liposomes have been developed for LIFU-responsive ultrasound imaging and targeted delivery of a STING agonist in breast cancer.<sup><a href="#cit0025" id="ref-cit0025">25</a></sup> These studies demonstrate the feasibility of PFP-mediated acoustic activation, CAT-mediated oxygen generation, and iRGD-mediated targeting, but they were developed with different payloads, tumor models, and therapeutic objectives.</p>
<p>Against this background, the present study focuses on integrating these complementary functions within a single liposomal nanodroplet platform for laryngeal cancer. We designed an iRGD-functionalized phase-change liposomal nanoplatform, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, to address these challenges through coordinated tumor targeting, ultrasound-responsive activation, chemotherapy, and hypoxia modulation. As illustrated in <a href="#sch0001" id="ref-sch0001">Scheme 1</a>, DOX serves as the chemotherapeutic payload, PFP acts as the ultrasound-responsive phase-transition core, CAT functions as an oxygen-generating component, and iRGD provides tumor-targeting capability. Rather than serving as independent additives, these components were integrated to provide complementary functions: iRGD-mediated tumor localization increases the local availability of the nanodroplets, PFP enables spatially controlled ultrasound activation and drug release, CAT modulates the hypoxic microenvironment, and DOX exerts the cytotoxic effect. To test this hypothesis, we systematically investigated its physicochemical properties, phase-transition behavior, ultrasound imaging performance, drug release characteristics, cellular uptake, in vitro cytotoxicity, hypoxia modulation, and in vivo therapeutic efficacy in a TU686 laryngeal cancer model.
<div id="sch0001" href="#ref-sch0001"></div><a name="sch0001" href="#ref-sch0001"></a><table class="thumbnail-table"><tbody><tr><td><a href="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_SCH0001g.jpg" class="float_border" target="_framename"><img alt="IRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP: ultrasound-guided laryngeal cancer therapy, targeting, phase change, drug release." longdesc="The diagram illustrates the mechanism of iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP for ultrasound imaging-guided therapy of laryngeal cancer. The nanosystem targets the laryngeal tumor site via iRGD binding to alphavbeta3/alphavbeta5 integrins on TU686 tumor cells. LIFU triggers a phase transition, enhancing imaging and promoting DOX release. PFP acts as the phase-transition core, while CAT decomposes endogenous H subscript 2 O subscript 2 into O subscript 2, alleviating hypoxia. The process involves chemotherapy and ultrasound-triggered drug release, with arrows indicating the flow of events and interactions." src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_SCH0001g_Thumb.jpg" class="imgsmall"></img></a></td><td>
<p class="tabtext"><strong>Scheme 1</strong> Schematic illustration of the proposed mechanism of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP for ultrasound imaging-guided therapy of laryngeal cancer. The nanosystem accumulates at the laryngeal tumor site through iRGD-mediated targeting, interacts with tumor cells, and undergoes LIFU-triggered phase transition. This process enhances ultrasound contrast, promotes DOX release, and, together with CAT-mediated decomposition of endogenous H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> into O<sub>2</sub>, alleviates tumor hypoxia and enhances the therapeutic effect. The dotted circle highlights the representative interaction region between iRGD on the nanodroplet and &#x03B1;v&#x03B2;3/&#x03B1;v&#x03B2;5 integrins on the TU686 tumor cell membrane, while dotted arrows indicate the release of functional components following ultrasound-triggered disruption of the nanodroplet.</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></p>
<h2>Materials and Methods</h2>
<h3>Materials and Chemicals</h3>
<p>Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC, CAS No.63&#x2013;89-8) and DSPE-PEG2000 (CAS No.1445775&#x2013;60-8) were purchased from Avanti (USA). Cholesterol (Chol, CAS No.57&#x2013;88-5) was obtained from Xi&#x2019;an Ruixi Biotechnology Co., Ltd. (Xi&#x2019;an, China). DSPE-PEG2000-iRGD (Cat. No.8007079&#x2013;2000) was purchased from Guangzhou Tanshtech Co., Ltd. (Guangzhou, China). DOX (98%, CAS No.23214&#x2013;92-8) and CAT (3500 U/mg, CAS No.9001&#x2013;05-2) were purchased from Shanghai Meclean Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). 4&#x2032;-6-Diamidino-2-phenylindole (DAPI, CAS No.28718&#x2013;90-3) was purchased from Beyotime Biotechnology (Shanghai, China). Calcein-AM/propidium iodide (PI) was obtained from Wuhan BaiQianDu Biotechnology Co., Ltd. (Wuhan, China). RPMI-1640 medium (Cat. No. G4535-500ML), fetal bovine serum (FBS, Cat. No. G8003-500ML), and the Cell Counting Kit-8 (CCK-8, Cat. No. G4103-5ML) were purchased from Wuhan Servicebio Technology Co., Ltd. (Wuhan, China). Anti-hypoxia-inducible factor-1&#x03B1; (HIF-1&#x03B1;, Cat. No.bs-0737R) rabbit polyclonal antibody and proliferating cell nuclear antigen (PCNA, Cat. No.bs-2007R) rabbit polyclonal antibody were purchased from Beijing Bioss Antibodies Co., Ltd. (Beijing, China). The Hypoxyprobe Plus Kit (HP2-100kit) was purchased from Shanghai Maokang Biotechnology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China).</p>
<h3>Synthesis of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP</h3>
<p>iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was prepared by the thin-film hydration method, followed by ultrasonic emulsification, with modifications from previously reported methods.<sup><a href="#cit0026" id="ref-cit0026">26</a></sup> DPPC, Chol, and DSPE-PEG2000-iRGD were dissolved in 5 mL of chloroform at a molar ratio of 65:30:1. Rotary evaporation was carried out at 42&#x00B0;C and 100 rpm for 2&#x00A0;h to remove the solvent and form a thin lipid film. The film was hydrated with 5 mL of PBS (pH 7.4) to obtain a lipid suspension. DOX solution (200&#x00A0;&#x00B5;L, 10 mg/mL), CAT solution (200&#x00A0;&#x00B5;L, 5 mg/mL), and PFP (200&#x00A0;&#x00B5;L) were mixed and pre-emulsified by probe sonication at 100 W for 3 min with a 5s on/5s off cycle. This emulsion was added to the lipid suspension, and the mixture was further sonicated at 125 W for 5 min with the same pulse cycle to obtain iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP. The dispersion was centrifuged at 8000 rpm for 5 min and washed three times with water to remove unencapsulated components. iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP were prepared using the same procedure. For the non-targeted formulation (lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP), DSPE-PEG2000 was used in place of DSPE-PEG2000-iRGD, while all other conditions were kept the same. The final formulations were stored at 4&#x00B0;C until use.</p>
<h3>Characterization of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP</h3>
<p>The morphology of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was examined by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Briefly, the freshly prepared formulations were appropriately diluted, and a 10&#x00A0;&#x03BC;L aliquot was deposited onto a carbon-coated copper grid. After adsorption for 1 min, excess liquid was removed with filter paper, and the samples were negatively stained with 1% phosphotungstic acid for 60s. After air drying, the samples were observed using a transmission electron microscope (Talos F200X, Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA) at an accelerating voltage of 200 kV. The hydrodynamic diameter, polydispersity index (PDI), zeta potential, and size distribution of the liposomes were measured by dynamic light scattering (DLS) using a Malvern Zetasizer Nano ZS (Malvern, USA) at 25&#x00B0;C. The stability of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP nanoparticles was also evaluated in PBS (0.01 M, pH 7.4) and DMEM containing 10% FBS over 7 days. The storage stability of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was evaluated at 4&#x00B0;C for 28 days. Samples were collected on days 0, 7, 14, and 28, and the hydrodynamic diameter, polydispersity index (PDI), and zeta potential were measured by dynamic light scattering. The amount of retained DOX in the formulation at each time point was quantified. The DOX content was determined by measuring the absorbance at 480 nm with a microplate reader (Lambda 25, PerkinElmer, USA). DOX retention was calculated according to the following equation:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0001" class="$eqn"><a name="um0001" href="#ref-um0001"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0001.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group></p>
<p>where <span id="ilm0001" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0001" href="#ref-ilm0001"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0001.jpg"></img></span> represents the amount of DOX retained in the nanodroplets immediately after preparation, and <span id="ilm0002" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0002" href="#ref-ilm0002"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0002.jpg"></img></span> represents the amount of DOX retained at the indicated storage time.</p>
<p>Drug loading (DL, %) was calculated as follows:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0002" class="$eqn"><a name="um0002" href="#ref-um0002"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0002.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group></p>
<p>where <span id="ilm0003" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0003" href="#ref-ilm0003"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0003.jpg"></img></span> represents the amount of DOX loaded into the nanoparticles, and <span id="ilm0004" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0004" href="#ref-ilm0004"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0004.jpg"></img></span> represents the total amount of nanoparticles.</p>
<p>Encapsulation efficiency (EE, %) was calculated according to the following equation:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0003" class="$eqn"><a name="um0003" href="#ref-um0003"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0003.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group></p>
<p>where <span id="ilm0005" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0005" href="#ref-ilm0005"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0005.jpg"></img></span> represents the amount of DOX retained in the recovered nanoparticles and <span id="ilm0006" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0006" href="#ref-ilm0006"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0006.jpg"></img></span> represents the total amount of DOX initially added during preparation.</p>
<h3>Evaluation of CAT Catalytic Activity</h3>
<p>The catalytic activity of CAT after encapsulation was evaluated using an ammonium molybdate-based spectrophotometric assay with minor modifications from previously reported methods.<sup><a href="#cit0027" id="ref-cit0027">27</a></sup> Briefly, free CAT, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, or CAT-free iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP was incubated with H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in PBS (pH 7.4) at 37&#x00B0;C. The amount of CAT in the free CAT and CAT-loaded nanodroplet groups was normalized to the same CAT-equivalent concentration. Free CAT and the CAT-loaded formulation were adjusted to an equivalent CAT concentration within the linear range of the assay. At predetermined time points (0, 1, 3, and 5 min), the reaction was terminated by addition of ammonium molybdate (32.4 mM). The absorbance of the resulting yellow complex was measured at 405 nm. A corresponding sample blank without H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> was included for each formulation to correct for background absorbance and light scattering from the nanodroplets. CAT-free iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP was used to determine whether the nanocarrier itself affected H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> consumption. Residual H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> concentrations were calculated from a standard calibration curve, and the percentage of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> decomposition was calculated as follows:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0004" class="$eqn"><a name="um0004" href="#ref-um0004"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0004.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group></p>
<p>where <span id="ilm0007" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0007" href="#ref-ilm0007"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0007.jpg"></img></span> and <span id="ilm0008" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0008" href="#ref-ilm0008"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0008.jpg"></img></span> represent the H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> concentrations at 0 min and at the indicated reaction time, respectively.</p>
<p>The oxygen-generating capacity of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was evaluated by directly monitoring dissolved oxygen using a dissolved oxygen meter, with minor modifications from previously reported methods.<sup><a href="#cit0027" id="ref-cit0027">27</a></sup> Phosphate-buffered saline (PBS, pH 7.4) was deoxygenated by bubbling with nitrogen before use to minimize differences in the initial oxygen concentration. Freshly prepared H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> was then added to reaction solutions containing H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> alone, CAT-free iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP, free CAT, or iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP. An additional iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP group was included to determine whether oxygen generation depended on the presence of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. The free CAT and CAT-loaded formulation were adjusted to equivalent CAT levels where appropriate. Immediately after H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> addition, the dissolved oxygen concentration was monitored using a dissolved oxygen meter (JPB-607A, Leici, China) at predetermined time points.</p>
<h3>In vitro Ultrasound Imaging of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP</h3>
<p>The ultrasound-responsive phase-transition behavior of the nanodroplets was evaluated with reference to previously reported PFP-based acoustic droplet vaporization systems.<sup><a href="#cit0011" id="ref-cit0011">11</a></sup> To directly observe the phase transition behavior, aliquots of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP were collected before and after 5 min of LIFU exposure (2s on/2s off, 2.5 W/cm<sup>2</sup>). Ultrasound irradiation was delivered using a WED-310 therapeutic ultrasound system (Shenzhen WELL.D Medical Electronics Co., Ltd., Shenzhen, China) equipped with a YDT-0101L hand-held transducer. The samples were placed on glass slides and examined under a light microscope.</p>
<p>The ultrasound-triggered phase transition and imaging stability of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP were also evaluated in vitro using an agar gel model. The agar gel was prepared by dissolving 3% agar in deionized water (w/v). Diluted iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was embedded in the agar gel, and LIFU was applied for 1, 2, 3, and 4 min. B-mode and contrast-enhanced ultrasound (CEUS) images were collected at each time point. Ultrasound signal intensity was quantified using DFY-II software.</p>
<h3>In vitro Ultrasound-Induced DOX Release</h3>
<p>In vitro DOX release was evaluated at pH 5.0 and 7.4 according to previously reported methods for DOX-loaded liposomes, with modifications.<sup><a href="#cit0028" id="ref-cit0028">28</a></sup> To evaluate the pH-dependent and ultrasound-triggered release of DOX from iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, freshly prepared iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP were dispersed in serum-containing PBS (10 g/L) at pH 5.0 or pH 7.4. Under each pH condition, the samples were divided into two equal-volume groups: a control group and a LIFU-treated group. The LIFU group was exposed to LIFU for 5 min, whereas the control group received no ultrasound treatment.</p>
<p>At 0, 1, 4, 6, 12, and 24&#x00A0;h, the samples were centrifuged, and 100&#x00A0;&#x03BC;L of the supernatant was collected from each sample. An equal volume of fresh release medium with the same pH and serum content was immediately added to maintain a constant volume. The amount of released DOX in the collected supernatant was quantified by measuring absorbance at 480 nm using a microplate reader. The cumulative release percentage was calculated as follows:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0005" class="$eqn"><a name="um0005" href="#ref-um0005"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0005.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group>.</p>
<h3>Cell Culture</h3>
<p>The human laryngeal cancer cell line TU686 was obtained from the Shanghai Institutes of Life Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences (Shanghai, China). The cells were cultured in DMEM supplemented with 10% (v/v) FBS, 100 U/mL penicillin, and 100 U/mL streptomycin. The cells were maintained at 37&#x00B0;C in a humidified incubator containing 5% CO<sub>2</sub>.</p>
<h3>Cellular Targeting Assay</h3>
<p>Cellular uptake and iRGD-mediated targeting were evaluated by confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) with reference to previously reported iRGD-functionalized liposome studies.<sup><a href="#cit0029" id="ref-cit0029">29</a></sup> TU686 cells were seeded in confocal dishes at a density of (5 &#x00D7; 10<sup>4</sup>) cells/mL and cultured for 24&#x00A0;h. The medium was then replaced with serum-free medium containing iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX, or lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP. The cells were incubated for 1, 2, or 4&#x00A0;h at 37&#x00B0;C. After incubation, the cells were washed with PBS to remove unbound formulations. The cells were then fixed with 4% paraformaldehyde for 10 min and washed three times with PBS. The nuclei were stained with DAPI (100&#x00A0;&#x03BC;L, 5 min). Cellular fluorescence was observed by CLSM (A1R-Si, Nikon, Tokyo, Japan).</p>
<h3>Confocal Observation of Intracellular DOX Release Under LIFU Irradiation</h3>
<p>To monitor intracellular DOX release under LIFU irradiation, FITC-labeled iRGD was used in place of iRGD during liposome preparation, while all other synthesis conditions were kept unchanged. TU686 cells were seeded in 35 mm glass-bottom confocal dishes and cultured for 24&#x00A0;h at 37&#x00B0;C. The cells were then incubated with FITC-iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP for 4&#x00A0;h. After incubation, one group was exposed to LIFU for 5 min, whereas the other group was kept under the same conditions without LIFU treatment. The medium was removed, and the cells were washed three times with PBS (pH 7.4). The nuclei were stained with DAPI (100&#x00A0;&#x03BC;L, 5 min). Intracellular localization of the carrier and DOX was observed by confocal laser scanning microscopy.</p>
<h3>Cell Viability Assay</h3>
<p>TU686 cells were seeded in 96-well plates at a density of 4&#x00D7;10<sup>3</sup> cells per well and cultured overnight to allow cell attachment. The cells were then incubated with iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX or iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP at final concentrations of 1.5, 3, 6, and 12&#x00A0;&#x03BC;g/mL. After incubation with the formulations, the cells were exposed to LIFU for 5 min or left untreated, and then cultured for an additional 24&#x00A0;h. Cell viability was measured using a CCK-8 according to the manufacturer&#x2019;s instructions and previously reported methods.<sup><a href="#cit0030" id="ref-cit0030">30</a></sup> Absorbance at 450 nm was recorded with a microplate reader (Synergy 4, BioTek, USA). Cell viability was calculated as follows:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0006" class="$eqn"><a name="um0006" href="#ref-um0006"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0006.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group></p>
<p>where <span id="ilm0009" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0009" href="#ref-ilm0009"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0009.jpg"></img></span> is the absorbance of the treated cells, <span id="ilm0010" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0010" href="#ref-ilm0010"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0010.jpg"></img></span> is the absorbance of the untreated control cells, and <span id="ilm0011" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0011" href="#ref-ilm0011"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0011.jpg"></img></span> is the absorbance of wells containing medium and CCK-8 but no cells.</p>
<h3>Live/Dead Cell Staining Assay</h3>
<p>TU686 cells were seeded in 24-well plates at a density of 2&#x00D7;10<sup>5</sup> cells per well and cultured overnight at 37&#x00B0;C. The medium was then replaced with fresh medium containing iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX or iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP at concentrations of 1.5, 3, 6, and 12&#x00A0;&#x03BC;g/mL. One group was exposed to LIFU for 5 min, while the other group received no ultrasound treatment. The cells were then further incubated for 24&#x00A0;h. After treatment, the cells were stained with Calcein-AM and PI for 30 min according to the manufacturer&#x2019;s instructions. Live and dead cells were observed by CLSM, with green fluorescence indicating viable cells and red fluorescence indicating dead cells.</p>
<h3>Experimental Animals and Ethical Approval</h3>
<p>Specific pathogen-free (SPF) male BALB/c nude mice (BALB/c-nu, 5&#x2013;8&#x00A0;weeks old) were purchased from the Guangdong Medical Laboratory Animal Center (Guangzhou, China). All animal procedures were approved by the Ethics Committee of the People&#x2019;s Hospital of Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region (Approval No. KY-GZR-2021-011) and were performed in accordance with institutional guidelines for the care and use of laboratory animals. For all in vivo procedures, mice were anesthetized with isoflurane in O<sub>2</sub> (4&#x2013;5% for induction and 0.5&#x2013;1.5% for maintenance). At the end of the experiments, the animals were euthanized by CO<sub>2</sub> inhalation, followed by cervical dislocation to confirm death, in accordance with the American Veterinary Medical Association (AVMA) Guidelines for the Euthanasia of Animals.</p>
<h3>Nude Mouse Xenograft Model of Laryngeal Cancer</h3>
<p>The TU686 xenograft model was established based on previously reported laryngeal cancer xenograft protocols,<sup><a href="#cit0017" id="ref-cit0017">17</a></sup> with minor modifications. 2&#x00D7;10<sup>6</sup> TU686 cells suspended in 100&#x00A0;&#x03BC;L of cell suspension were injected subcutaneously into the dorsal flank of each mouse. Tumor growth was monitored by measuring the longest diameter (<span id="ilm0012" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0012" href="#ref-ilm0012"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0012.jpg"></img></span>) and the shortest diameter (<span id="ilm0013" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0013" href="#ref-ilm0013"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0013.jpg"></img></span>) with calipers every 3 days. Tumor volume was calculated using the following formula:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0007" class="$eqn"><a name="um0007" href="#ref-um0007"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0007.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group></p>
<p>where <span id="ilm0014" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0014" href="#ref-ilm0014"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0014.jpg"></img></span> represents tumor volume, <span id="ilm0015" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0015" href="#ref-ilm0015"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0015.jpg"></img></span> is the longest tumor diameter, and <span id="ilm0016" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0016" href="#ref-ilm0016"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0016.jpg"></img></span> is the diameter perpendicular to <span id="ilm0017" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0017" href="#ref-ilm0017"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0017.jpg"></img></span>. When the tumor volume reached approximately 100 mm<sup>3</sup>, the mice were randomly assigned to different treatment groups for subsequent experiments.</p>
<h3>In vivo Fluorescence Imaging and Biodistribution</h3>
<p>In vivo fluorescence imaging and biodistribution were evaluated using DiR-labeled liposomes with reference to previously reported iRGD-functionalized liposome studies.<sup><a href="#cit0025" id="ref-cit0025">25</a></sup> TU686 xenograft-bearing mice were randomly divided into three groups (n = 3 per group): DiR-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, DiR-iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, and the iRGD-blocking group. Mice in the first two groups were intravenously injected via the tail vein with the corresponding DiR-labeled formulations at a dose of 6 mg/kg. In the blocking group, free iRGD (12 mg/kg) was administered intravenously 1&#x00A0;h before injection of DiR-iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP (6 mg/kg).</p>
<p>Whole-body fluorescence images were acquired at 0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 12, and 24&#x00A0;h after administration using an in vivo fluorescence imaging system (PE IVIS Spectrum, PerkinElmer, USA). Identical acquisition parameters were used for all groups and time points. Fluorescence intensity in the tumor region was quantified by region-of-interest (ROI) analysis using the imaging software. At 24&#x00A0;h after administration, the mice were euthanized, and the tumors and major organs, including the heart, liver, spleen, lungs, and kidneys, were collected for ex vivo fluorescence imaging. Fluorescence intensity in each tissue was quantified using the same imaging software.</p>
<h3>In vivo Ultrasound-Triggered Phase Transition and Contrast Imaging</h3>
<p>Tumor-bearing mice were intravenously injected via the tail vein with iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX or iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP at a dose of 6 mg/kg. Based on the in vivo fluorescence biodistribution study, in which the tumor-associated fluorescence of DiR-iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP reached its maximum at 6&#x00A0;h after intravenous administration, LIFU irradiation was performed at this time point. The tumor region was exposed to LIFU for 10 min. CEUS imaging was then performed using a Mindray ultrasound system (Mindray, China) equipped with an L3-12 linear-array transducer and operated in contrast mode.<sup><a href="#cit0031" id="ref-cit0031">31</a></sup> Dynamic CEUS images of the tumor region were acquired and recorded in real time. Ultrasound signal intensity was quantified by manually delineating ROIs within the tumor and analyzing the corresponding gray-scale values using the system software before and after LIFU irradiation.</p>
<h3>Evaluation of Tumor Hypoxia Alleviation</h3>
<p>Tumor hypoxia was evaluated by pimonidazole staining and HIF-1&#x03B1; immunofluorescence according to established methods, with minor modifications.<sup><a href="#cit0032" id="ref-cit0032">32</a></sup> TU686 xenograft-bearing nude mice were treated when the tumor volume reached approximately 100 mm<sup>3</sup>. The mice were randomly divided into five groups: PBS (control), free DOX, lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU, iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP + LIFU, and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU. LIFU (2.5 W/cm<sup>2</sup>) irradiation was applied for 10 min once every 3 days for 15 days. At the end of the treatment period, pimonidazole hydrochloride (30 mg/kg) was administered by intraperitoneal injection. After 90 min, the mice were euthanized, and the tumors were harvested for immunofluorescence analysis. Tumor cryosections were incubated with an anti-HIF-1&#x03B1; primary antibody at 4&#x00B0;C overnight and then incubated with a CoraLite Plus 555-labeled goat anti-rabbit secondary antibody. Pimonidazole HCl and HIF-1&#x03B1;-related fluorescence signals were observed by CLSM. Hypoxic areas were semi-quantified using ImageJ by calculating the ratio of the positively stained area to the total tumor section area, as follows:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0008" class="$eqn"><a name="um0008" href="#ref-um0008"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0008.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group></p>
<p>where <span id="ilm0018" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0018" href="#ref-ilm0018"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0018.jpg"></img></span> represents the area showing positive fluorescence staining and <span id="ilm0019" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0019" href="#ref-ilm0019"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0019.jpg"></img></span> represents the total tumor section area.</p>
<p>To further quantify HIF-1&#x03B1; protein expression, tumor tissues from the different treatment groups were subjected to Western blot analysis. Total proteins were extracted from tumor tissues, and protein concentrations were determined using a BCA protein assay. Equal amounts of protein were separated by SDS-PAGE and transferred onto PVDF membranes at a constant current of 350 mA for 30 min. After blocking, the membranes were incubated overnight at 4&#x00B0;C with primary antibodies against HIF-1&#x03B1; (Servicebio, GB154936, 1:200) and GAPDH (Servicebio, GB15004, 1:10,000). The membranes were then incubated with HRP-conjugated secondary antibody (Servicebio, GB23303, 1:3000). Protein bands were visualized using an enhanced chemiluminescence detection system. Band intensities were quantified using ImageJ software, and HIF-1&#x03B1; expression was normalized to GAPDH.</p>
<h3>In vivo Antitumor Study</h3>
<p>TU686 xenograft-bearing nude mice were randomly assigned to eight groups (n = 5 per group): PBS (vehicle control), free DOX, lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP, lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX + LIFU, iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP + LIFU, and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU. The PBS group served as the vehicle control, free DOX represented conventional chemotherapy, the non-iRGD formulation was used to assess the effect of active targeting, and the CAT-free formulation was used to evaluate the role of oxygen modulation. The PFP-containing formulation with LIFU irradiation was compared with non-phase-change formulations to determine the contribution of ultrasound-triggered phase transition. For the groups receiving ultrasound treatment, LIFU (2.5 W/cm<sup>2</sup>, 10 min) was applied to the tumor region 6&#x00A0;h after intravenous administration, corresponding to the time point of maximal tumor accumulation determined by in vivo fluorescence imaging. This treatment was performed once every 3 days for 18 days. Tumor size and body weight were recorded every 3 days throughout the treatment period. Tumor volume was calculated using the following formula:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0009" class="$eqn"><a name="um0009" href="#ref-um0009"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0009.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group></p>
<p>where <span id="ilm0020" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0020" href="#ref-ilm0020"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0020.jpg"></img></span> is the longest tumor diameter and <span id="ilm0021" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0021" href="#ref-ilm0021"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0021.jpg"></img></span> is the diameter perpendicular to <span id="ilm0022" class="$ieqn"><a name="ilm0022" href="#ref-ilm0022"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_ILM0022.jpg"></img></span>.</p>
<p>On day 18, the mice were euthanized. The tumors, heart, liver, spleen, lungs, and kidneys were excised and fixed in 4% paraformaldehyde for histological analysis. The tissues were subsequently embedded in paraffin, sectioned, and stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&#x0026;E). Histopathological changes were examined under a light microscope to evaluate potential treatment-related organ toxicity. Tumor sections were stained for PCNA to evaluate tumor cell proliferation. Blood samples were collected from mice in each group and allowed to clot, followed by centrifugation to obtain serum. Serum alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and aspartate aminotransferase (AST) levels were measured to evaluate hepatic function, while blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and serum creatinine (CRE) levels were determined to assess renal function.</p>
<h3>Hemolysis Assay</h3>
<p>The hemocompatibility of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was evaluated using a red blood cell hemolysis assay with reference to established methods for nanoparticle hemocompatibility assessment.<sup><a href="#cit0033" id="ref-cit0033">33</a></sup> Fresh anticoagulated mouse blood was centrifuged, and the erythrocytes were washed three times with PBS and resuspended to obtain a 2% erythrocyte suspension. Equal volumes of the erythrocyte suspension and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP at different concentrations (25, 50, 100, 200, and 400&#x00A0;&#x03BC;g/mL) were mixed and incubated at 37&#x00B0;C for 4&#x00A0;h. PBS and deionized water were used as the negative and positive controls, respectively. The samples were then centrifuged at 3000 rpm for 5 min, and the absorbance of the supernatant was measured at 540 nm. Because the formulation contained DOX, a corresponding material blank without erythrocytes was prepared at each concentration to correct for formulation-derived absorbance. The hemolysis rate was calculated as follows:
<disp-formula-group>
<p id="um0010" class="$eqn"><a name="um0010" href="#ref-um0010"></a><img src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_UM0010.jpg"></img></p>
</disp-formula-group></p>
<p>where the absorbance of each nanoparticle-containing sample was corrected using its corresponding material blank.</p>
<h3>Statistical Analysis</h3>
<p>Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS 26.0 (IBM, USA). Data are presented as mean &#x00B1; standard deviation (SD). Comparisons between two independent groups were performed using a two-tailed independent-samples <em>t</em>-test; paired comparisons were used when measurements were obtained from the same experimental unit before and after treatment. For experiments involving more than two independent groups with a single experimental factor, one-way ANOVA was performed, followed by Tukey&#x2019;s multiple-comparison test when pairwise comparisons among groups were required or Dunnett&#x2019;s test when the prespecified comparisons were made against a single control group. For experiments involving two experimental factors, two-way ANOVA was used with multiplicity-adjusted post hoc comparisons. A <em>p</em> value &#x003C; 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Statistical significance was indicated as follows: &#x002A;<em>p</em> &#x003C; 0.05, &#x002A;&#x002A;<em>p</em> &#x003C; 0.01, &#x002A;&#x002A;&#x002A;<em>p</em> &#x003C; 0.001, ns indicates no significant difference.</p>
<h2>Results and Discussion</h2>
<h3>Synthesis and Characterization of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP</h3>
<p>iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was fabricated from DPPC, DSPE-PEG2000-iRGD, and Chol by thin-film hydration followed by ultrasonic emulsification, enabling the co-encapsulation of DOX, CAT, and PFP. TEM was performed to directly examine the morphology of the prepared nanodroplets. As shown in <a href="#f0001" id="ref-f0001">Figure 1a</a> and <a href="#f0001" id="ref-f0001">b</a>, both iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP exhibited well-defined, approximately spherical nanostructures with relatively uniform morphology. DLS showed that iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP had a mean hydrodynamic diameter of 236.10 &#x00B1; 9.28 nm, a PDI of 0.21 &#x00B1; 0.03, and a zeta potential of &#x2212;31.42 &#x00B1; 0.69 mV (<a href="#f0001" id="ref-f0001">Figure 1c</a> and <a href="#f0001" id="ref-f0001">d</a>). The corresponding iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX formulation displayed a comparable particle size of 223.05 &#x00B1; 6.09 nm, a slightly lower PDI of 0.17 &#x00B1; 0.03, and a similar surface charge of &#x2212;30.22 &#x00B1; 0.06 mV. These results indicate that incorporation of PFP did not markedly alter the overall colloidal characteristics of the liposomal system, although it caused a modest increase in particle diameter and dispersity. The relatively uniform size distribution indicates successful formation of stable liposomal nanodroplets. Particle size is a critical parameter affecting biodistribution, circulation behavior, and tumor accumulation of nanomedicines. Previous studies have demonstrated that nanosystems within the sub-200&#x2013;300 nm range generally exhibit favorable biological behavior for tumor delivery.<sup><a href="#cit0034" id="ref-cit0034">34</a>,<a href="#cit0035" id="ref-cit0035">35</a></sup> PEGylation further improves colloidal behavior by increasing surface hydrophilicity and providing steric shielding, which can reduce opsonization and uptake by the mononuclear phagocyte system, thereby prolonging blood circulation.<sup><a href="#cit0036" id="ref-cit0036">36</a></sup> The moderately negative zeta potential observed here may also help maintain dispersion stability by limiting particle aggregation. Together, these physicochemical features support the suitability of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP as a tumor-directed nanocarrier.
<div id="f0001" href="#ref-f0001"></div><a name="f0001" href="#ref-f0001"></a><table class="thumbnail-table"><tbody><tr><td><a href="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0001g.jpg" class="float_border" target="_framename"><img alt="Different plots and micrographs of iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP." longdesc="Image A: TEM image of iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX with a 500 nm scale bar. Image B: TEM image of iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP, also with a 500 nm scale bar. Image C: Histogram showing size distribution (1-10000 nm) with intensity (0-20 percent). Bars cluster between 100-1000 nm, peaking at 200-300 nm with 15-16 percent intensity. Image D: Bar chart of zeta potential (mV) ranging from -40 to 0. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX is near -30 mV and iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP is near -32 mV, marked as &#x2032;ns&#x2032;. Image E: Line graph of size over time (days 0-8) with error bars. Size (150-350 nm) remains between 220-255 nm from days 1 to 7 for 4 degrees C storage, PBS (pH 7.4) and DMEM. Image F: Bar chart of DL (percent) ranging from 0 to 10. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX is about 7.5 percent and iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP is about 5 percent, marked with an asterisk." src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0001g_Thumb.jpg" class="imgsmall"></img></a></td><td>
<p class="tabtext"><strong>Figure 1</strong> (<strong>a</strong>) TEM image of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX. (<strong>b</strong>) TEM image of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP. (<strong>c</strong>) Hydrodynamic size distribution of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP. No significant difference was found between the two groups. (<strong>d</strong>) Zeta potential of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP. (<strong>e</strong>) Time-dependent changes in the hydrodynamic diameter of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP at 4&#x00B0;C and in PBS (pH 7.4) or DMEM over 7 days. (<strong>f</strong>) DOX DL of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP. Data are presented as mean &#x00B1; SD (n = 3). &#x002A;<em>P</em> &#x003C; 0.05; ns, not significant.</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></p>
<p>The DL and EE of DOX in iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP were 4.95 &#x00B1; 0.98% and 24.75 &#x00B1; 4.90%, respectively, both lower than those of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX (7.35 &#x00B1; 0.55% and 36.75 &#x00B1; 2.75%, respectively) (<a href="#f0001" id="ref-f0001">Figure 1f</a>). These results indicate that incorporation of PFP decreased DOX retention during formulation preparation, possibly by altering the available loading space and lipid packing during emulsification. The storage stability of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was further evaluated at 4&#x00B0;C over 28 days. As shown in <u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S1</a></u>, the hydrodynamic diameter increased only slightly from 236.10 &#x00B1; 9.28 nm on day 0 to 251.92 &#x00B1; 7.65 nm on day 28. The PDI remained within a narrow range, varying from 0.21 &#x00B1; 0.03 to 0.24 &#x00B1; 0.04, while the zeta potential remained relatively stable, ranging from &#x2212;31.42 &#x00B1; 0.69 mV to &#x2212;30.98 &#x00B1; 0.31 mV. These results indicate that no obvious aggregation or marked surface-charge variation occurred during storage. DOX retention gradually decreased over time, from 100.00 &#x00B1; 0.00% at day 0 to 97.23 &#x00B1; 2.34% at day 7, 93.45 &#x00B1; 4.42% at day 14, and 86.45 &#x00B1; 5.82% at day 28, indicating limited DOX leakage during prolonged storage. Nevertheless, the formulation retained the majority of the encapsulated DOX after 28 days. The relatively high retention may be partly attributed to the presence of cholesterol in the lipid bilayer, which can enhance membrane packing and reduce premature leakage of encapsulated compounds from liposomal systems.<sup><a href="#cit0037" id="ref-cit0037">37</a>,<a href="#cit0038" id="ref-cit0038">38</a></sup> These findings demonstrate that iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP possesses good physicochemical stability and drug-retention capacity for at least 28 days under refrigerated storage conditions.</p>
<h3>Catalytic Activity of CAT After Encapsulation</h3>
<p>Because the preparation of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP involves ultrasonic emulsification, it was necessary to determine whether CAT retained its catalytic function after incorporation into the nanodroplets. CAT activity was therefore evaluated by monitoring H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> decomposition using the ammonium molybdate assay, which has been widely applied to assess the catalytic activity of CAT incorporated into lipid- and polymer-based nanocarriers. As shown in <a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">Figure 2a</a>, the H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> concentration remained largely unchanged in the H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> control and CAT-free iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP groups during the incubation period, indicating that the nanocarrier components themselves exhibited negligible H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>-decomposing activity. In contrast, both free CAT and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP induced a rapid, time-dependent decrease in residual H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. After 5 min of incubation, free CAT and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP decomposed approximately 61.61 &#x00B1; 6.87% and 34.27 &#x00B1; 5.48% of the initial H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, respectively. The measurable H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>-decomposing activity of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP demonstrates that CAT remained catalytically functional after incorporation into the liposomal nanodroplets. If the activity of encapsulated CAT was lower than that of an equivalent amount of free CAT, this difference may be associated with partial loss of enzyme activity during formulation and/or restricted accessibility of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> to CAT within the lipid structure.
<div id="f0002" href="#ref-f0002"></div><a name="f0002" href="#ref-f0002"></a><table class="thumbnail-table"><tbody><tr><td><a href="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0002g.jpg" class="float_border" target="_framename"><img alt="A composite figure with three line graphs and microscopy and ultrasound image sequences for LIFU effects." longdesc="Image A: Line graph showing H subscript 2O subscript 2 decomposition over time (min). Free CAT decomposes from 13 percent at 1 min to 61 percent at 5 min. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP decomposes from 4 percent at 1 min to 34 percent at 5 min. iRGD-lipo at DOX-PFP remains near 0 percent. Image B: Optical microscopy views before and after LIFU, highlighting circular droplets. Image C: B mode and CEUS views over time (0-4 min), with a scale bar indicating intensity. Image D: Line graph showing cumulative release (percent) over time (h). iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX ranges from 6 percent at 1h to 15 percent at 24h. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP ranges from 12 percent at 1h to 27 percent at 24h. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX plus LIFU ranges from 8 percent at 1h to 16 percent at 24h. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU ranges from 33 percent at 1h to 61 percent at 24h. Image E: Similar graph with cumulative release (percent) over time (h). iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX ranges from 4 percent at 1h to 12 percent at 24h. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP ranges from 7 percent at 1h to 21 percent at 24h. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX plus LIFU ranges from 5 percent at 1h to 13 percent at 24h. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU ranges from 13 percent at 1h to 26 percent at 24h." src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0002g_Thumb.jpg" class="imgsmall"></img></a></td><td>
<p class="tabtext"><strong>Figure 2</strong> (<strong>a</strong>) H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>-decomposing activity of free CAT, iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP at different times. (<strong>b</strong>) Optical microscopy images of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP before and after LIFU irradiation. Arrows indicate representative nanodroplets. (<strong>c</strong>) Representative B-mode and CEUS images of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP acquired during LIFU irradiation for 0&#x2013;4 min. The color bar indicates the relative CEUS signal intensity, from low to high. (<strong>d</strong>) Cumulative DOX release profiles of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP at pH 5.0 in the presence or absence of LIFU. (<strong>e</strong>) Cumulative DOX release profiles of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP at pH 7.4 in the presence or absence of LIFU. Data are presented as mean &#x00B1; SD (n = 3).</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></p>
<p>Having confirmed that CAT retained its H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>-decomposing activity after encapsulation, we next examined whether this catalytic reaction resulted in measurable O<sub>2</sub> generation. Dissolved oxygen was monitored directly after addition of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> to the different formulations. As shown in <u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S2</a></u>, the dissolved oxygen concentration remained nearly unchanged in the H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> control and CAT-free iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP groups throughout the measurement period. Similarly, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP in the absence of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> did not produce an appreciable increase in dissolved oxygen, indicating that O<sub>2</sub> generation depended on the availability of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> as the catalytic substrate. In contrast, addition of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> to iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP caused a rapid increase in dissolved oxygen concentration from 0 to 54.17 &#x00B1; 9.62 mg/L within 5 min. Free CAT produced a comparable rapid increase in dissolved oxygen under the same conditions. CAT remained functionally active after incorporation into the nanodroplets and was capable of converting H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> into O<sub>2</sub>. These results are crucial for CAT to achieve its goal of modulating the hypoxic tumor microenvironment on the current nanoscale platform.</p>
<h3>In vitro Phase-Transition Behavior and Ultrasound Imaging Performance of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP</h3>
<p>PFP is a low-boiling-point perfluorocarbon that can undergo a liquid-to-gas transition when exposed to ultrasound above the vaporization threshold. As a result, PFP-loaded nanoparticles can be converted into echogenic microbubbles, which enhance ultrasound contrast through acoustic droplet vaporization (ADV).<sup><a href="#cit0039" id="ref-cit0039">39</a></sup> Besides signal enhancement, this phase transition may also improve local retention by increasing particle size and acoustic responsiveness at the irradiated site. The phase-transition behavior of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was first examined by optical microscopy after LIFU exposure (2.5 W/cm<sup>2</sup>, 2s on/2s off, 5 min). As shown in <a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">Figure 2b</a>, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP became markedly enlarged and generated abundant microbubbles after irradiation, which is consistent with ADV-mediated vaporization of the PFP core. ADV-generated microbubbles provide strong acoustic impedance mismatch, resulting in enhanced ultrasound scattering. Similar mechanisms have been reported in ultrasound-responsive perfluorocarbon nanodroplets.<sup><a href="#cit0040" id="ref-cit0040">40</a></sup> Because phase-change droplets show little inherent contrast before activation but become highly scattering after vaporization, this result provides direct visual evidence that the encapsulated PFP remained acoustically responsive within the liposomal structure.<sup><a href="#cit0041" id="ref-cit0041">41</a></sup> In line with this observation, both B-mode and CEUS signals increased with irradiation time (<a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">Figure 2c</a>). A slight enhancement in echo intensity was detected after 1 min. The B-mode signal reached its maximum at 2 min, while the CEUS signal peaked at 3 min (<u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S3</a></u>), and then gradually decreased. This pattern is in line with the dynamic behavior of ADV systems, in which progressive droplet vaporization initially amplifies acoustic scattering, whereas prolonged insonation can reduce the number of intact droplets available for further activation and may also destabilize newly formed bubbles.<sup><a href="#cit0011" id="ref-cit0011">11</a></sup> These results confirm that PFP was successfully incorporated into iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP.</p>
<h3>In vitro DOX Release from iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP</h3>
<p>We next examined the pH- and LIFU-dependent release behavior of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP by varying the external medium. DOX release was measured at pH 5.0 and pH 7.4, with or without LIFU irradiation, and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX without PFP was used as the control. As shown in <a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">Figure 2d</a> and <a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">e</a>, the PFP-containing formulation released more DOX than the PFP-free control at both pH values, and this difference became more evident after LIFU exposure. This result suggests that PFP plays an important role in promoting drug release. Because PFP has a low boiling point, a fraction of the encapsulated droplets may undergo partial vaporization even under mild conditions, which can increase membrane tension and facilitate DOX leakage from the liposomal core. After LIFU irradiation, DOX release from iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP increased further and remained clearly higher than that from iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX, especially at pH 5.0 (<a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">Figure 2d</a>). This behavior is consistent with an ultrasound-triggered phase-transition mechanism. Under acoustic stimulation, PFP undergoes a liquid-to-gas transition, which rapidly increases intraparticle pressure and drives liposome expansion. The resulting mechanical stress can disrupt the phospholipid shell, generate transient membrane defects, or even cause partial rupture, thereby accelerating DOX diffusion into the surrounding medium.<sup><a href="#cit0042" id="ref-cit0042">42</a></sup> These data agree well with the phase-transition and imaging results shown in <a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">Figure 2b</a> and <a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">c</a>, where LIFU-induced vaporization enhanced ultrasound contrast and confirmed the acoustic responsiveness of the formulation.</p>
<p>The release profiles also showed a clear pH dependence. Both formulations released more DOX at pH 5.0 than at pH 7.4, regardless of whether ultrasound was applied. This trend may be related to the intraliposomal distribution behavior of DOX. Because DOX is hydrophilic, its dissolution in the aqueous core can lower the internal pH of the liposome.<sup><a href="#cit0043" id="ref-cit0043">43</a></sup> When the external medium remains near neutral, the resulting pH gradient may favor DOX retention within the acidic core and thereby reduce diffusion-driven release. Under mildly acidic conditions, this retention effect is weakened, leading to greater drug release. Importantly, tumor acidity is spatially and temporally heterogeneous rather than uniformly distributed throughout the tumor mass, owing to regional differences in perfusion, oxygenation, and metabolic activity.<sup><a href="#cit0044" id="ref-cit0044">44</a></sup> Such heterogeneity may limit delivery systems that rely exclusively on local acidity, because drug release can vary among tumor regions with different extracellular pH values. Tumor pH heterogeneity has been increasingly recognized as an important source of variability in pH-responsive therapeutic strategies.<sup><a href="#cit0045" id="ref-cit0045">45</a></sup> In the present system, this limitation may be partially mitigated by combining endogenous pH-dependent DOX release with externally controlled ultrasound activation. While acidic conditions weaken DOX retention and promote drug release, ultrasound induces the liquid-to-gas transition of PFP and mechanically perturbs the liposomal structure, thereby providing an additional release mechanism that is not solely dependent on the local extracellular pH. Consequently, ultrasound activation may promote drug release even in tumor regions where acidity is insufficient to strongly accelerate passive pH-dependent release. This complementary combination of endogenous and exogenous triggering mechanisms reduces reliance on a single heterogeneous tumor microenvironmental cue and provides greater spatiotemporal control over drug release.</p>
<h3>Cellular Uptake and Ultrasound-Responsive Intracellular DOX Delivery of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP</h3>
<p>Efficient cellular internalization is essential for nanocarrier-mediated chemotherapy. CLSM confirmed the targeting effect of iRGD. Among the tested incubation times, TU686 cells treated with iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP for 4&#x00A0;h exhibited the strongest intracellular red fluorescence (<u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S4</a></u>), indicating that this time point was favorable for cellular accumulation. As shown in <a href="#f0003" id="ref-f0003">Figure 3a</a>, strong DOX-derived red fluorescence was observed in TU686 cells treated with iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP or iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX, indicating efficient cellular association and internalization. In contrast, free DOX and the non-targeted formulation (lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP) produced much weaker fluorescence, consistent with limited intracellular accumulation. The relatively low intracellular retention of free DOX may be attributed to rapid efflux mediated by membrane drug transporters, inefficient passive diffusion, and the absence of receptor-mediated uptake, whereas nanoparticle-based delivery facilitates endocytosis and improves intracellular drug retention. Notably, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP produced stronger intracellular DOX fluorescence than iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX, which agrees with the release data and suggests that PFP facilitates DOX release and may promote endosomal escape.<sup><a href="#cit0012" id="ref-cit0012">12</a></sup> These results indicate that iRGD modification effectively enhances the targeted uptake of liposomal nanodroplets by TU686 cells. After binding to integrins, proteolytic exposure of the CendR motif enables interaction with neuropilin-1, promoting nanoparticle internalization and tissue penetration.<sup><a href="#cit0016" id="ref-cit0016">16</a></sup>
<div id="f0003" href="#ref-f0003"></div><a name="f0003" href="#ref-f0003"></a><table class="thumbnail-table"><tbody><tr><td><a href="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0003g.jpg" class="float_border" target="_framename"><img alt="Fluorescence micrograph with DAPI/DOX stained cells, two labels and four fluorescence intensity graphs." longdesc="" src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0003g_Thumb.jpg" class="imgsmall"></img></a></td><td>
<p class="tabtext"><strong>Figure 3</strong> (<strong>a</strong>) CLSM images and fluorescence intensity line-scan profiles of intracellular uptake of free DOX, lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX, and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP in TU686 cells (scale bar = 100&#x00A0;&#x00B5;m). (<strong>b</strong>) CLSM images of TU686 cells treated with iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP with or without LIFU irradiation (scale bar = 50&#x00A0;&#x00B5;m). Blue: DAPI; Green: FITC-labeled iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP; Red: DOX.</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></p>
<p>Because efficient cellular accumulation alone is not sufficient for optimal chemotherapy, we next examined whether ultrasound could further promote intracellular DOX delivery from the accumulated nanodroplets. Ultrasound offers a noninvasive external trigger with controllable penetration depth and good spatiotemporal precision.<sup><a href="#cit0046" id="ref-cit0046">46</a></sup> It can therefore overcome limitations associated with tumor pH heterogeneity<sup><a href="#cit0047" id="ref-cit0047">47</a></sup> and avoid the collateral damage often associated with thermal triggering strategies.<sup><a href="#cit0048" id="ref-cit0048">48</a></sup> As shown in <a href="#f0003" id="ref-f0003">Figure 3b</a>, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP accumulated around TU686 cells, which is consistent with the targeting function of iRGD. Without LIFU irradiation, DOX release remained limited during the short incubation period, and most fluorescence was still confined to the carrier. After LIFU exposure, intranuclear DOX fluorescence increased markedly, indicating rapid drug release and efficient intracellular transport. This behavior is consistent with ultrasound-triggered PFP vaporization, in which ADV increases intraparticle pressure, induces liposome expansion and/or shell disruption, and accelerates DOX release. Since iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP had already accumulated around tumor cells before irradiation, this triggered release occurred in close proximity to the target cells, thereby increasing the local DOX concentration and enhancing cytotoxic delivery.</p>
<h3>In vitro Antitumor Effects</h3>
<p>The in vitro antitumor activity of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was evaluated in TU686 cells with or without LIFU irradiation. As shown in <a href="#f0004" id="ref-f0004">Figure 4a</a>, in the absence of LIFU, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX produced only limited growth inhibition, and cell viability remained relatively high across the tested concentration range. Under the same conditions, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP showed stronger cytotoxicity than iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX at matching concentrations, suggesting that the incorporation of PFP facilitated a higher baseline level of intracellular DOX release, which is consistent with the release behavior observed in <a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">Figure 2d</a> and <a href="#f0002" id="ref-f0002">e</a>. The difference between the two formulations became more pronounced after LIFU irradiation. In particular, at 6 and 12&#x00A0;&#x03BC;g/mL, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP combined with LIFU produced the lowest cell viability among all treatment groups, and this advantage became increasingly evident with increasing concentration. This trend was further supported by dose-response analysis. As shown in <a href="#f0004" id="ref-f0004">Figure 4b</a>, the IC<sub>50</sub> value of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP was 3.27&#x00A0;&#x03BC;g/mL, which was markedly lower than that of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX (11.27&#x00A0;&#x03BC;g/mL). The live/dead staining results in <a href="#f0004" id="ref-f0004">Figures 4c</a>, <u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">S5</a></u>, and S<u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">6</a></u> were fully consistent with the CCK-8 data and provided direct visual evidence of the treatment effect. In the absence of LIFU, cells in both treatment groups remained predominantly Calcein-AM positive, with only limited PI staining, indicating relatively weak cytotoxicity. After LIFU exposure, however, the iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP group showed a clear increase in red fluorescence and a marked decrease in green fluorescence, especially at higher concentrations, confirming extensive cell death. These findings suggest that LIFU-triggered phase transition of PFP promotes intracellular DOX release and thereby amplifies the antitumor effect of the nanodroplets.
<div id="f0004" href="#ref-f0004"></div><a name="f0004" href="#ref-f0004"></a><table class="thumbnail-table"><tbody><tr><td><a href="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0004g.jpg" class="float_border" target="_framename"><img alt="Images of TU686 cells: viability, inhibition ratio and CLSM under various treatments." longdesc="The image A shows a bar graph of cell viability (percent) against concentration (micrograms per milliliter) for iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX, iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP, iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX plus LIFU and iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU. Significant differences are marked with and ns. The image B shows a line graph of inhibition ratio (percent) against log concentration (micrograms per milliliter) for iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP, with IC50 values of 11.27 micrograms per milliliter and 3.27 micrograms per milliliter, respectively. The image C shows CLSM images of calcein-AM/PI-stained TU686 cells treated with iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX or iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP at concentrations of 1.5, 3, 6 and 12 micrograms per milliliter, with and without LIFU irradiation. The scale bar is 100 micrometers." src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0004g_Thumb.jpg" class="imgsmall"></img></a></td><td>
<p class="tabtext"><strong>Figure 4</strong> (<strong>a</strong>) Cell viability of TU686 cells after 24&#x00A0;h under different treatments and concentrations. (<strong>b</strong>) Dose&#x2013;response curves and corresponding IC50 values of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP. (<strong>c</strong>) CLSM images of calcein-AM/PI-stained TU686 cells after treatment with iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX or iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP at 1.5, 3, 6, and 12&#x00A0;&#x03BC;g/mL, with or without LIFU irradiation (scale bar = 100&#x00A0;&#x00B5;m). Data are presented as mean &#x00B1; SD (n = 6). &#x002A;<em>P</em> &#x003C; 0.05, &#x002A;&#x002A;<em>P</em> &#x003C; 0.01, &#x002A;&#x002A;&#x002A;<em>P</em> &#x003C; 0.001; ns, not significant.</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></p>
<h3>In vivo Tumor Targeting and Biodistribution of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP</h3>
<p>To directly evaluate the contribution of iRGD modification to tumor accumulation in vivo, DiR-labeled nanodroplets were monitored by longitudinal near-infrared fluorescence imaging. Following intravenous administration, the tumor-associated fluorescence signal of DiR-iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP gradually increased over time and became clearly distinguishable from the surrounding tissue (<a href="#f0005" id="ref-f0005">Figure 5a</a>). Quantitative ROI analysis further showed that the tumor fluorescence intensity reached its maximum at 6&#x00A0;h and subsequently decreased at 12 and 24&#x00A0;h (<a href="#f0005" id="ref-f0005">Figure 5b</a>). Accordingly, 6&#x00A0;h after intravenous administration was selected as the time point for subsequent LIFU irradiation, when tumor accumulation of the iRGD-functionalized nanodroplets was maximal. To further examine whether the enhanced tumor accumulation was associated with iRGD-mediated targeting, a competitive blocking experiment was performed by administering free iRGD before the iRGD-functionalized nanodroplets. Notably, pretreatment with free iRGD markedly reduced the tumor fluorescence signal of DiR-iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, and the signal in the blocking group remained weak throughout the observation period (<a href="#f0005" id="ref-f0005">Figure 5a</a> and <a href="#f0005" id="ref-f0005">b</a>). These findings support the contribution of iRGD-mediated recognition to the enhanced tumor accumulation of the nanodroplets rather than passive accumulation alone. A substantial fluorescence signal was also observed in the hepatic region, particularly in the non-targeted and blocking groups. Such hepatic distribution is consistent with the known clearance of intravenously administered lipid nanoparticles by the mononuclear phagocyte system, with the liver representing a major site of nanoparticle sequestration. Similar hepatic accumulation has been reported for systemically administered DiR-labeled liposomes.<sup><a href="#cit0049" id="ref-cit0049">49</a></sup>
<div id="f0005" href="#ref-f0005"></div><a name="f0005" href="#ref-f0005"></a><table class="thumbnail-table"><tbody><tr><td><a href="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0005g.jpg" class="float_border" target="_framename"><img alt="Two plots of near infrared fluorescence over time, with a peak around 6&#x00A0;hours in one group." longdesc="Image A displays near-infrared fluorescence images of mice at seven time points: 0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 12 and 24&#x00A0;hours. Rows are labeled as DiR-lipo atCAT-DOX-PFP, DiR-iRGD-lipo at CAT- DOX-PFP and iRGD-blocking-group. Fluorescence is concentrated in the torso and tumor regions. A vertical scale indicates intensity from high to low. Image B is a line graph showing tumor fluorescence intensity over time for three groups. The x-axis is labeled &#x2032;Time (hours)&#x2032; from 0 to 24 and the y-axis is &#x2032;Total radiant efficiency&#x2032; ranging from 0 to 1.5 x 10 superscript 10. The legend includes DiR-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP, DiR-iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP and iRGD-blocking-group. DiR-iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP increases from 0 at 0&#x00A0;hours to peak at 8.5 x 10 superscript 9 at 6&#x00A0;hours, then declines to 5.0 x 10 superscript 9 at 24&#x00A0;hours. DiR-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP and iRGD-blocking-group remain near baseline throughout." src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0005g_Thumb.jpg" class="imgsmall"></img></a></td><td>
<p class="tabtext"><strong>Figure 5</strong> (<strong>a</strong>) Whole-body NIR fluorescence images at 0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 12, 24&#x00A0;h (n = 3). (<strong>b</strong>) Tumor ROI fluorescence intensity versus time. Data are presented as mean &#x00B1; SD (n = 3).</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></p>
<p>Ex vivo fluorescence imaging of the excised tumors and major organs at 24&#x00A0;h further corroborated the whole-body imaging results. The tumor from the DiR-iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP group exhibited a stronger fluorescence signal than those from the non-targeted and blocking groups, whereas prominent fluorescence was detected in the liver in all groups (<u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S7</a></u>). Taken together, these data provide direct in vivo evidence that iRGD modification promotes the tumor localization of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP.</p>
<h3>In vivo Ultrasound Imaging of LIFU-Triggered Phase Transition</h3>
<p>Given the strong ADV-mediated contrast enhancement observed in vitro, we next assessed the in vivo imaging performance of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP in mice bearing subcutaneous TU686 xenografts. As shown in <u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S8</a></u>, CEUS signals in the tumor region remained weak 6&#x00A0;h after intravenous injection of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX, and no obvious signal enhancement was observed after LIFU exposure. Similarly, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP alone produced only limited contrast in the absence of ultrasound activation. In contrast, when tumors treated with iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP were exposed to LIFU (2.5 W/cm<sup>2</sup>, 10 min), clear patchy hyperechoic signals appeared in the tumor region in both grayscale and CEUS images (<a href="#f0006" id="ref-f0006">Figure 6</a>). This result indicates that the iRGD-functionalized nanodroplets were able to accumulate in the tumor and remained acoustically activatable after systemic administration. Upon LIFU irradiation, the encapsulated PFP underwent phase transition and generated gas-filled microbubbles, which markedly increased local acoustic scattering and thereby enhanced ultrasound contrast in the irradiated tumor area. The absence of comparable signal enhancement in the non-PFP control group further supports that the observed contrast amplification originated from phase transition of the PFP core rather than from nonspecific ultrasound effects alone. Quantitative ROI analysis of signal intensity in the tumor region further confirmed the imaging observations (<a href="#f0006" id="ref-f0006">Figure 6b</a>). The present imaging behavior is consistent with previous iRGD-functionalized PFP-containing phase-transition liposomes, in which LIFU-induced vaporization enhanced ultrasound contrast and promoted localized payload release. For example, iRGD-STING-PFP liposomes have been reported to undergo LIFU-responsive phase transition and improve ultrasound molecular imaging in breast cancer models.<sup><a href="#cit0025" id="ref-cit0025">25</a></sup> In the present system, however, the phase-change mechanism was coupled directly with DOX chemotherapy and CAT-mediated hypoxia modulation, extending this strategy toward combined imaging-guided chemotherapy and tumor microenvironment regulation in laryngeal cancer.
<div id="f0006" href="#ref-f0006"></div><a name="f0006" href="#ref-f0006"></a><table class="thumbnail-table"><tbody><tr><td><a href="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0006g.jpg" class="float_border" target="_framename"><img alt="Ultrasound images and graph of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP treatments." longdesc="The image A shows ultrasound imaging of TU686 xenografts. The top row displays 2D and CEUS images for iRGD-lipo atCAT-DOX-PFP treatment, showing weak signals. The bottom row shows 2D and CEUS images for iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU treatment, with enhanced signals in the tumor region indicated by a dashed circle. The image B shows a bar graph comparing grayscale intensity (a.u.) for iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP and iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU treatments, with the latter showing significantly higher intensity, marked by three asterisks indicating p-value less than 0.001." src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0006g_Thumb.jpg" class="imgsmall"></img></a></td><td>
<p class="tabtext"><strong>Figure 6</strong> (<strong>a</strong>) In vivo ultrasound imaging of TU686 xenografts in grayscale and CEUS mode after different treatments. The white dashed circle indicates the tumor region. (<strong>b</strong>) Quantitative analysis of ultrasound signal intensity in the tumor region after different treatments. Data are presented as mean &#x00B1; SD (n = 3). &#x002A;&#x002A;&#x002A;<em>P</em> &#x003C; 0.001.</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></p>
<h3>CAT-Mediated Alleviation of Tumor Hypoxia</h3>
<p>To determine whether CAT-loaded lipid nanoparticles could relieve tumor hypoxia, hypoxic status was evaluated by immunofluorescence staining of pimonidazole HCl, an exogenous hypoxia probe, and HIF-1&#x03B1;, an endogenous hypoxia marker. As shown in <a href="#f0007" id="ref-f0007">Figure 7a</a>, the PBS group exhibited strong pimonidazole and HIF-1&#x03B1; fluorescence, indicating a pronounced hypoxic state within the tumor tissue. For pimonidazole staining, both the free DOX group and the lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU group showed lower fluorescence than the PBS group, indicating partial improvement in tumor oxygenation. The iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP + LIFU group, however, did not produce a comparable reduction and retained relatively strong pimonidazole positivity. In contrast, the iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU group exhibited the weakest green fluorescence in the tumor sections and the lowest quantified pimonidazole signal (<a href="#f0007" id="ref-f0007">Figure 7a</a> and <a href="#f0007" id="ref-f0007">b</a>), indicating the most effective suppression of hypoxic regions. Importantly, the quantified pimonidazole signal in this group was significantly lower than that in the free DOX group, the lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU group, and the iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP + LIFU group, supporting a clear advantage of the targeted CAT-containing formulation under ultrasound activation. A similar trend was observed for HIF-1&#x03B1; (<a href="#f0007" id="ref-f0007">Figure 7a</a> and <a href="#f0007" id="ref-f0007">c</a>). Pimonidazole accumulation reflects intracellular hypoxia because it forms adducts under low oxygen conditions. Reduced staining after CAT-containing treatment suggests improved oxygen availability. Catalase-mediated decomposition of endogenous H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> into oxygen has been widely explored as an oxygen-generating strategy to relieve tumor hypoxia.<sup><a href="#cit0050" id="ref-cit0050">50</a></sup> Notably, the iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP + LIFU group showed relatively high HIF-1&#x03B1; intensity despite lacking CAT, suggesting that ultrasound-triggered drug release alone was insufficient to relieve hypoxia and may not counteract hypoxia-associated stress within the tumor. The likely mechanism is that CAT decomposes endogenous H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> into O<sub>2</sub>.<sup><a href="#cit0051" id="ref-cit0051">51</a></sup> CAT-mediated oxygen generation has also been incorporated into liposomal or enzyme-based nanoplatforms to relieve tumor hypoxia and enhance therapeutic response. For example, catalase-containing liposomes co-delivering DOX have been shown to improve tumor oxygenation and strengthen combination therapy.<sup><a href="#cit0052" id="ref-cit0052">52</a></sup> In comparison, the present platform combines this oxygen-generating function with iRGD-mediated targeting and LIFU-triggered PFP phase transition, thereby linking hypoxia modulation with externally controlled drug release and ultrasound imaging within the same formulation.
<div id="f0007" href="#ref-f0007"></div><a name="f0007" href="#ref-f0007"></a><table class="thumbnail-table"><tbody><tr><td><a href="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0007g.jpg" class="float_border" target="_framename"><img alt="Five-panel figure showing tumor hypoxia markers Pimonidazole HCl and HIF-1 alpha across five treatment groups." longdesc="A composite scientific figure with five panels comparing tumor hypoxia markers across five treatment groups: PBS, Free DOX, lipo atCAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU, iRGD-lipo at DOX-PFP plus LIFU and iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU. Panel A shows immunofluorescence microscopy of tumor sections stained with DAPI, Pimonidazole HCl and HIF-1 alpha, with a merged view. PBS shows the strongest signals in both Pimonidazole HCl and HIF-1 alpha columns. iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU shows the weakest signals across both stains. Scale bar equals 100 micrometers. Panel B is a bar chart of Pimonidazole HCl relative fluorescence intensity with y-axis ranging 0 to 150. PBS is highest at approximately 95, iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU is lowest at approximately 5 and Free DOX, lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU and iRGD-lipo at DOX-PFP plus LIFU fall between approximately 30 and 45. Significance brackets marked with 4 asterisks and 2 asterisks indicate p less than 0.0001 and p less than 0.01 respectively. Panel C is a bar chart of HIF-1 alpha relative fluorescence intensity with y-axis ranging 0 to 200. iRGD-lipo at DOX-PFP plus LIFU is the highest at approximately 95, iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU is lowest at approximately 15 and PBS, Free DOX and lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU range approximately 45 to 60. Significance brackets indicate p less than 0.0001 and p less than 0.001. Panel D shows a Western blot with HIF-1 alpha and GAPDH rows across five lanes. HIF-1 alpha bands are strongest in Free DOX, lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU, and iRGD-lipo at DOX-PFP plus LIFU lanes and weakest in iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU. GAPDH bands appear consistent across all lanes. Panel E is a bar chart of relative HIF-1 alpha expression with y-axis ranging 0.0 to 0.9. lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU is highest at approximately 0.45, iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU is lowest at approximately 0.05 and PBS and Free DOX are approximately 0.12. Significance brackets indicate p less than 0.0001." src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0007g_Thumb.jpg" class="imgsmall"></img></a></td><td>
<p class="tabtext"><strong>Figure 7</strong> (<strong>a</strong>) Representative immunofluorescence images of tumor sections from TU686 xenograft-bearing mice after different treatments. Pimonidazole HCl staining (green) indicates hypoxic regions, HIF-1&#x03B1; staining (red) indicates hypoxia-related protein expression, and nuclei are stained with DAPI (blue). Scale bar = 100&#x00A0;&#x03BC;m. (<strong>b</strong>) Quantitative analysis of pimonidazole fluorescence intensity in tumor sections after the indicated treatments. (<strong>c</strong>) Quantitative analysis of HIF-1&#x03B1; fluorescence intensity in tumor sections after the indicated treatments (n = 5). (<strong>d</strong>) Representative Western blot bands of HIF-1&#x03B1; and GAPDH. (<strong>e</strong>) Densitometric analysis of HIF-1&#x03B1;/GAPDH. Data are presented as mean &#x00B1; SD (n = 3). &#x002A;&#x002A;<em>P</em> &#x003C; 0.01, &#x002A;&#x002A;&#x002A;<em>P</em> &#x003C; 0.001, and &#x002A;&#x002A;&#x002A;&#x002A;<em>P</em> &#x003C; 0.0001; ns, not significant.</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></p>
<p>To further substantiate the changes in HIF-1&#x03B1; observed by immunofluorescence, HIF-1&#x03B1; protein expression in tumor tissues was quantified by Western blotting. As shown in <a href="#f0007" id="ref-f0007">Figure 7d</a>, the PBS group exhibited a strong HIF-1&#x03B1; band, consistent with the pronounced hypoxic state observed by pimonidazole staining. HIF-1&#x03B1; expression was partially reduced in the free DOX group, whereas the iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP + LIFU and lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU groups retained relatively high HIF-1&#x03B1; expression. In contrast, the iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU group showed the lowest HIF-1&#x03B1; protein level. Densitometric analysis of the HIF-1&#x03B1;/GAPDH ratio further confirmed this trend (<a href="#f0007" id="ref-f0007">Figure 7e</a>). These results provide additional quantitative evidence that the targeted CAT-containing nanodroplets effectively attenuated tumor hypoxia under LIFU activation.</p>
<h3>In vivo Antitumor Efficacy of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP</h3>
<p>The antitumor effect of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP combined with LIFU was further evaluated using TU686 tumor-bearing mice (<a href="#f0008" id="ref-f0008">Figure 8a</a>). Representative photographs of mice at days 0, 9, and 18 are presented in <a href="#f0008" id="ref-f0008">Figure 8b</a>, and the excised tumors collected on day 18 are presented in <u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S9</a></u>. The PBS group showed the fastest tumor growth, whereas free DOX slowed tumor progression only to a limited extent. The lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP and iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP groups without LIFU also showed moderate tumor inhibition, but their effects remained clearly weaker than those of the corresponding ultrasound-treated groups. After LIFU activation, both lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU and iRGD-lipo@DOX-PFP + LIFU suppressed tumor growth more effectively, indicating that ultrasound-triggered drug release contributed substantially to the therapeutic response. Notably, the iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU group displayed the slowest tumor growth and the lowest final tumor volume, demonstrating that the combination of tumor targeting, oxygen-generating catalysis, phase-transition responsiveness, and ultrasound activation produced the strongest therapeutic benefit. By comparison, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX + LIFU also inhibited tumor growth, but the effect was weaker than that of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU, highlighting the importance of PFP-mediated phase transition in amplifying the in vivo therapeutic effect. The tumor growth curves in <a href="#f0008" id="ref-f0008">Figure 8c</a> further supports the visual observations.
<div id="f0008" href="#ref-f0008"></div><a name="f0008" href="#ref-f0008"></a><table class="thumbnail-table"><tbody><tr><td><a href="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0008g.jpg" class="float_border" target="_framename"><img alt="A schematic and graphs showing treatment effects on TU686 xenograft-bearing mice over 18 days." longdesc="The schematic illustration shows the treatment schedule for TU686 xenograft-bearing mice, starting with tumor inoculation on day -7, followed by treatment with intravenous injection on day 0, LIFU after 6&#x00A0;hours and continued treatment every three days until euthanasia and sample collection on day 18. Photographs of mice from each treatment group are shown on days 0, 9 and 18, labeled as PBS, Free DOX, lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP, iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP, lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU, iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX plus LIFU, iRGD-lipo at DOX-PFP plus LIFU and iRGD-lipo at CAT-DOX-PFP plus LIFU. A graph displays tumor volume in cm superscript 3 over time in days, showing different treatment effects. Another graph shows body weight in grams over time in days. Representative PCNA staining of tumor sections collected from each group on day 18 is shown, with dashed circles indicating areas of interest. Scale bar equals 100 micrometers." src="article/fulltext_file/616689/aW1n/IJN_A_616689_O_F0008g_Thumb.jpg" class="imgsmall"></img></a></td><td>
<p class="tabtext"><strong>Figure 8</strong> (<strong>a</strong>) Schematic illustration of the treatment schedule for TU686 xenograft-bearing mice. (<strong>b</strong>) Representative photographs of mice from each treatment group on days 0, 9, and 18. (<strong>c</strong>) Tumor growth curves of TU686 xenografts under different treatments. (<strong>d</strong>) Body weight changes of mice during the treatment period. (<strong>e</strong>) Representative PCNA staining of tumor sections collected from each group on day 18. Dashed circles indicate representative areas of interest. Scale bar = 100&#x00A0;&#x03BC;m. Data are presented as mean &#x00B1; SD (n = 5). &#x002A;&#x002A;&#x002A;<em>P</em> &#x003C; 0.001.</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table></p>
<p>Body weight changes are shown in <a href="#f0008" id="ref-f0008">Figure 8d</a>. No obvious treatment-related body-weight loss was observed during the treatment period. Because body weight alone provides only a general indication of tolerability, systemic biosafety was further evaluated by hemolysis testing, serum biochemical analysis, and histological examination of major organs, as described below. As shown in <u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S10</a></u>, no visible hemolysis was observed after incubation of erythrocytes with iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP over the tested concentration range. Quantitative analysis showed that the hemolysis rate remained below 5% even at the highest concentration tested (400&#x00A0;&#x03BC;g/mL), indicating minimal disruption of erythrocyte membranes. Serum ALT and AST levels showed no significant differences between the iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP-treated groups and the PBS control. Similarly, BUN and creatinine levels remained comparable among the treatment groups, indicating no detectable impairment of renal function under the present treatment conditions (<u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S11</a></u>). Histological examination of the major organs further supported these findings. H&#x0026;E-stained sections of the heart, liver, spleen, lungs, and kidneys showed preserved tissue architecture without obvious treatment-related necrosis, inflammatory infiltration, or structural injury (<u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S12</a></u>). Together with the absence of obvious body-weight loss during treatment, these results indicate acceptable short-term systemic biocompatibility of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP under the tested conditions. Nevertheless, the long-term fate of PFP warrants further investigation, as perfluorocarbon clearance may depend on formulation characteristics and administered dose, with possible tissue retention before eventual pulmonary elimination.<sup><a href="#cit0053" id="ref-cit0053">53</a></sup> Future studies should therefore assess the long-term biodistribution, clearance, and repeated-dose safety of PFP-containing nanodroplets.</p>
<p>Histological analysis further confirmed these findings. PCNA is a nuclear protein involved in DNA replication and is commonly used as an indicator of cellular proliferative activity.<sup><a href="#cit0054" id="ref-cit0054">54</a></sup> Strong PCNA staining therefore reflects a higher proportion of actively proliferating tumor cells, whereas reduced PCNA positivity indicates suppression of tumor cell proliferation. As shown in <a href="#f0008" id="ref-f0008">Figure 8e</a>, PCNA staining was strongest in the PBS group, indicating active tumor cell proliferation. Free DOX and the nanoparticle groups without ultrasound reduced PCNA positivity to some extent, but the decrease was limited. In contrast, the ultrasound-treated groups exhibited markedly weaker PCNA staining, and the iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU group showed the lowest PCNA signal, indicating the strongest suppression of tumor cell proliferation. This trend was further validated by the semi-quantitative analysis in <u><a href="https://www.dovepress.com/article/supplementary_file/616689/616689%20Supplementary%20Material.docx">Figure S13</a></u>, in which the positive area ratio of PCNA was lowest in the iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP + LIFU group and was significantly lower than that in the PBS, free DOX, and most other treatment groups. The quantitative PCNA data therefore agree well with the tumor photographs and growth curves, and reinforce the conclusion that this formulation achieved the most effective inhibition of tumor progression.</p>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>In summary, we developed an iRGD-functionalized phase-change liposomal nanoplatform, iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP, that integrates tumor targeting, LIFU-responsive phase transition, controlled DOX release, and CAT-mediated hypoxia modulation. The nanodroplets exhibited suitable physicochemical characteristics and effective uptake by TU686 cells. Upon LIFU irradiation, PFP underwent acoustic droplet vaporization, resulting in enhanced ultrasound contrast and accelerated DOX release. CAT retained its catalytic activity after encapsulation and generated O<sub>2</sub> through H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> decomposition, which was associated with reduced tumor hypoxia. In vivo, iRGD modification enhanced tumor accumulation, and the complete formulation combined with LIFU produced the strongest tumor growth inhibition among the tested groups. These findings support the potential of iRGD-lipo@CAT-DOX-PFP as an ultrasound imaging-guided therapeutic platform that combines spatially controlled chemotherapy with hypoxia modulation for laryngeal cancer. Despite these encouraging findings, several limitations should be considered before clinical translation. First, the therapeutic efficacy was evaluated using a subcutaneous TU686 xenograft model, which does not fully reproduce the anatomical and microenvironmental characteristics of laryngeal tumors. Second, the pharmacokinetics, long-term toxicity, and repeated-dose safety of the formulation remain to be further investigated. Optimization and standardization of LIFU parameters will also be necessary for clinical application. Overall, these findings support the further development of this ultrasound-responsive nanoplatform for imaging-guided laryngeal cancer therapy.</p>
</div>
